---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy, Uploads, Retention, and Analytics | compressimage.fun"
  description="Which tools upload, which stay in the browser, four-hour deletion, Delete now, logs, Google Analytics, and optional Umami. No no-upload claim for server jobs."
  canonicalPath="/privacy"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Privacy', path: '/privacy' },
  ]}
>
  <header class="hero compact">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">Plain-language privacy</div>
      <h1>Your files are temporary. Some tools never receive them.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Server tools need the image to do the work. Browser-only tools do not upload it. Analytics,
        when built into the static site, never receives the file bytes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <article class="article shell">
    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="article-summary">
        Files processed on compressimage.fun servers are automatically deleted within four hours.
        You can delete a completed job immediately. Do not treat a server compressor as a no-upload
        product.
      </p>
      <section>
        <h2>Server tools (these upload)</h2>
        <p>
          Compression, exact-size presets, batch ZIP, resize, crop, rotate, format conversion,
          metadata inspect/remove, form-photo and signature preparation, watermarks, and favicon
          generation upload images to the same-origin processing API. Each temporary job uses a
          cryptographically random ID and a separate high-entropy access token. Files are stored
          under random internal names, not your filename.
        </p>
        <p>
          The source, outputs, small private previews, and safe job metadata live in an isolated
          processor volume. A cleanup task runs at startup and periodically. Delete now removes the
          complete job directory immediately.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Browser tools (these do not upload the file)</h2>
        <p>
          Image to Base64, Base64 to Image, Base64 Image Viewer, Image to Data URI, Image to PDF,
          and Image Color Picker run in browser memory. Their file or pasted value is not sent to
          the processing API. They still load ordinary page assets and, if analytics is enabled,
          page-view scripts.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Logs</h2>
        <p>
          Operational logs may contain a request ID, tool ID, format, coarse size bucket, duration,
          and a controlled error category. They do not intentionally contain image bytes, Base64
          data, GPS values, access tokens, result URLs, or full original filenames.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Analytics</h2>
        <p>
          The public site may include Google Analytics 4 when <code>PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID</code>
          is present at Docker build time. Production inspected on 22 August 2026 loaded
          measurement ID <code>G-W530RG17S3</code> with <code>anonymize_ip</code> and
          <code>allow_google_signals: false</code>. That is usage analytics, not file processing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Umami loads only when <code>PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID</code> is set at build time. Local
          Docker builds without those variables do not inject analytics scripts. The typed event
          layer sends only controlled categories such as tool ID, format, file-count bucket, and
          processing result. Filenames, image contents, EXIF, job IDs, access tokens, result URLs,
          and free-form errors are excluded.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a measurement ID is built in, a page view can still tell Google or Umami that a public
          URL was opened, along with ordinary browser and network metadata those products collect.
          That is not the same as storing your image. It is also not “analytics off.”
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>What metadata removal cannot do</h2>
        <p>
          Removing EXIF and GPS fields does not hide information visible in the pixels. Faces,
          signs, reflections, and document text may still reveal private details.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
